The superseded record's diagnosis was RIGHT about the residual (+0x1C cmpwi r29,0; +0x20 bge +0x28; +0x24 unconditional b +0x58 vs our blt) and WRONG about the mechanism: it concluded 'the pre-peephole CFG canonicalizer folds the whole pair ... before the peephole-disabled backend can retain target's branch-to-next plus b', i.e. that the shape was source-unreachable. Three new classes measured here:
PROBE 1 (REGRESSED 26 insns/real 9): bare goto SEQUENCE, not if/else -- `if (texidx >= 0) {
    goto init;
}
goto done;
init:` -- collapses identically to the if/else forms the cap already killed, so the goto is the folding trigger regardless of if/else framing.
PROBE 2 (REGRESSED real 2 -> 18, insns T27/O29): scoped `#pragma peephole off` around the function with the natural guard `if (texidx < 0) {
    goto done;
}`. This REFUTES the peephole reading outright: peephole-off changed `mr.` to `mr`+`cmplwi`, `addi rX,rY,0` to `mr`, and added two spurious `b`, yet STILL emitted the fused `blt`. -O4 has peephole ON by default (configure.py cflags_demo comment, measured 2026-09-01).
PROBE 3 (REGRESSED 26 insns/real 9): loop-exit branch class -- `do {
    if (texidx < 0) {
        break;
    }
    for (...) {...}
} while (0);`
PROBE 4 (EXACT): `if (seq == NULL || texidx < 0) {
    return;
}
for (i = 0; i < seq->ntexmods; i++) {
    tm = (TEXMOD*)((char*)seq->texmods + i * 0x58);
    InitTexMod(tm, texidx);
}`
